course: everyday
lesson: 198
title: Telling when someone may be lying
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: There is no magic sign of a lie. Noses do not grow like Pinocchio's, and looking away does not prove anything: shy, nervous or autistic people often avoid eye contact while telling the complete truth.
explain: Some things are clues worth checking, like a story that changes every time it is told, or a claim that sounds too amazing to be true. A clue is a reason to ask gentle questions or check another way, not proof.
explain: Many untrue stories are not lies: memory fills its gaps by guessing, so two honest people can remember the same fight differently. And a fish «as big as a car» is just a story growing in the telling.
explain: Example: a boy in Florida swears «cross my heart» that he saw a shark in the pool. Swearing harder does not make it truer. What counts is the facts, and over time what people do, not how often they say «trust me».
fact: There is no single body sign that proves someone is lying.
fact: Avoiding eye contact does not prove a lie; shy, nervous or autistic people may do it while telling the truth.
fact: A story whose details keep changing is a clue to check gently, not proof, because people do forget details.
fact: Swearing hard, like «cross my heart», does not make a statement more true.
fact: Unusual claims need good evidence before you believe them.
fact: Calling someone a liar without proof is unfair and unkind.
fact: Lie-detector machines (polygraphs) measure nervousness, like heartbeat and sweat, and honest people get nervous too, so they are not reliable.
fact: Memory fills gaps by guessing, so honest people can remember the same event differently.
fact: People often remember events in their own favour without meaning to lie.
fact: Young children often lie out of fear, and kindness makes honesty easier for them.
fact: Trust is earned by actions over time, not by saying «trust me».
fact: Someone who gets angry at a question may be upset for other reasons.
exercise: A friend will not look you in the eye while telling a story. What can you conclude? | Nothing for sure, since shy or nervous people look away too | He is definitely lying to you | He is definitely telling the truth | He must be hiding something bad that he does not want you to find out
exercise: A classmate's story about losing her lunch money changes each time. What is fair? | Notice it and ask gently, without accusing her | Call her a liar in front of the class | Ignore it, since a true story never changes even a little | Tell the teacher she stole the money
exercise: Someone says «On my mother's life, it's true!» Does that make it true? | No, a liar can swear just as easily as an honest person | Yes, because nobody would ever swear on their mother's life falsely | Yes, if he says it loudly enough | Only if he says it three times
exercise: A boy says he caught a fish as big as a car. How should you take it? | As exaggeration, with a smile | As a serious lie to report to a teacher | As a true fact about big fish in lakes | As proof he is a dangerous person
exercise: A story sounds too perfect and full of amazing details. What is wise? | Ask simple questions or check before believing it | Believe it, because details make it true | Refuse ever to listen to that person again, since it must be made up | Spread it, because it is so exciting
exercise: A friend says she met a famous footballer. What is a fair response? | Be curious or enjoy the story, without calling her a liar | Tell her she is lying because it is unlikely | Demand a photo of them together, or tell her you will not be friends | Tell everyone she makes things up
exercise: Someone gets angry and changes the subject when asked a simple question. What should you remember? | He might be upset for other reasons, so stay calm and fair | It proves he is lying about everything else he has told you as well | Angry people always tell the truth | You should get angry back at him
exercise: Why are lie-detector machines not good proof? | They measure nervousness, and honest people get nervous too | They can only be used on adults | They always say everyone is lying | They only ever work in films and on TV, never in real life at all
exercise: What is true about Pinocchio's growing nose? | It is a story; there is no magic sign of a lie | Real noses grow a tiny bit when people lie, but too little to see | Noses only grow when you tell big lies | Doctors use nose size to check lies
exercise: Two friends tell you different versions of a fight. What is wise? | Listen to both and ask an adult to help if it matters | Believe the friend you like best | Decide that both of them are liars | Pick the version that sounds more exciting, since that is probably true
exercise: A small child says he did not eat the chocolate, but his face is covered in it. What helps most? | Stay gentle and help him tell the truth | Shout at him until he finally admits it | Laugh at him for being so silly and messy | Tell him he can never have chocolate again
exercise: You remember a holiday differently from your dad. Is one of you lying? | Not necessarily; memory can be wrong even when nobody lies | Yes, the person who is wrong is lying | Yes, adults never remember things wrongly, so it must be the child | Yes, children's memories are always wrong
exercise: A boy keeps saying «trust me». How should you judge whether to trust him? | By what he does over time | By how often he says «trust me» | By how loudly and firmly he says it | By whether he looks you in the eye
test: A shy new girl in Wellington looks at the floor when she says she has a cat. What is the fair thought? | Looking away is not proof, so there is no reason to doubt her | She is clearly lying about the cat | Shy people usually lie more | You should ask her to look at you and repeat it
test: Mia says she was at the beach on Saturday, then the cinema, then her gran's. What is a sensible reaction? | Ask kindly what she did, as the changes are worth checking | Decide she lies about everything | Tell her friends not to trust her | Pretend not to notice anything at all and change the subject quickly
test: A man on TV says a pill makes you taller in a week and swears it really works. What should you think? | An amazing claim needs good evidence, not swearing | If he swears, it must be true | TV adverts are always checked to be true | It works for some people, so it must be fine for everybody to take
test: A boy is nervous in a polygraph test and the machine says «lying». Why might it be wrong? | Honest people get nervous too, and the machine measures nerves | Polygraphs only work on very small children | The machine was switched off during the test | Machines are always right, so it cannot be wrong
test: Two brothers in Cork each say the other started the argument. What is most likely? | Each remembers it in his own favour without meaning to lie | One of them is a terrible liar | Both are lying on purpose to trick you into taking their side | Nobody started it, so it did not happen
test: Your grandad remembers your birthday picnic happening in the rain; you remember sun. What is the best explanation? | Memory fills gaps by guessing, so honest people can disagree | Grandad is telling a lie to tease you | You are telling a lie to win the argument | Only one person can ever have a true memory of it, so one must be lying
test: A classmate says «trust me» all the time but often does not do what he says. What does this tell you? | His actions matter more than his words | He must be trustworthy, since he says so | Saying «trust me» makes promises count double | You should trust him even more, just to be fair
test: A girl gets cross when asked where her homework is. What should the teacher remember? | She may be upset for another reason, so stay calm and fair | Getting cross always means someone is lying | Getting cross always means someone is innocent and should be left alone | It is best to get cross with her too
test: Which is the best clue that a story should be checked? | It keeps changing each time it is told | The person speaks quietly | The person looks away from you while they are talking | The person blinks a lot
test: he wont look at me so he's def lying, right? | No, looking away doesn't prove a lie | Yes, liars always look away when they talk | Yes, and he's scratching his nose too | Yes, eye contact is proof of the truth
test: she swore cross my heart so it must be true | No, swearing hard doesn't make it more true | Yes, nobody would ever lie after saying cross my heart | Yes, that's a real promise | Only if she said it twice
test: me and my bro remember the trip totally different, one of us is lyin | Not necessarily, honest memories differ | Yes, one must be lying | Yes, the younger one lies | Yes, the louder one is lying
test: kid says he met a famous footballer at the shop, believe him? | Wait for good evidence before believing it | Yes, he wouldn't say it otherwise | No, call him a liar in front of everyone so he learns | Yes, if he says trust me
explain: A boy in Texas believed an online seller who said a game console was real and cheap; he sent his birthday money and got nothing back.
explain: A girl in Leeds believed a stranger who said her mum was in hospital and he would drive her there; she told a teacher first, and the teacher found out it was a lie.
fact: People who lie to trick you often rush you, offer something too good to be true, or ask you to keep a secret.
fact: Checking with a trusted adult before acting on a surprising story stops many tricks.
test: online guy selling a console for 20 quid, pay now or it's gone | Don't pay, rushing and too-cheap means it's likely a scam | Pay fast before it's gone, deals like that never come back twice | Pay half first | Send your card details
test: stranger says ur mum's hurt, come with me now | Don't go, tell a trusted adult and check | Go with him quickly | Ask him to show you a picture of her first, then go with him | Go if he knows your name
test: how can u tell someone might be lying to trick you | They rush you or offer something too good to be true | They are always very polite and smile a lot when they talk to you | They speak quietly | They wear smart clothes
test: did u hear Sam never looks u in the eye, my sister says that proves he's lying about everything | No; avoiding eye contact proves nothing, shy or nervous people do it too | Yes, no eye contact always means lying | Only liars look at the floor | Tell everyone Sam's a liar
test: apparently a man outside the school told Lucy her mum was in hospital n tried to get her in his car, they say he had a knife | Never go with someone who says that; tell a teacher and check; the knife may be rumour | Go if he knows your mum's name | Ask to see his car first | It's fine if he looks friendly
test: my mate swears "cross my heart" he saw a shark in the leisure centre pool, so it must be true innit | No; swearing harder doesn't make it true, unusual claims need evidence | Yes, swearing proves it | Call him a liar in front of everyone | Believe it if he swears twice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the evidence that a person is lying? | No single body sign proves that someone is lying | Avoiding eye contact proves that someone is lying | Swearing "cross my heart" proves one tells the truth | A lie detector reliably shows who is lying
exam: Identify the features commonly displayed by a person who lies in order to trick you. | They rush you, offer too much, or ask for secrecy | They speak slowly and give you time to think | They encourage you to ask your parents first | They offer nothing and make no requests at all
exam: It is given that two honest persons remember the same event differently. Determine the most probable explanation. | Memory fills its gaps by guessing | One of them must be lying on purpose | Both of them must be lying on purpose | The event can never have happened at all
exam: Ascertain the step that most reliably defeats a trick based on a surprising story. | Checking with a trusted adult before acting | Acting quickly before the chance is gone | Asking the stranger to promise it is true | Keeping the story a secret from everyone
